<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qatif</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qatif is an oasis town and port on the Gulf coast of eastern Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> islands. Long the main harbour of the al-Ahsa region, it was held in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, Uyunids, Jarwanids and Jabrids, and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the Banu Khalid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> described its Shi'a Arab population around 1331, and it remains a centre of Saudi Arabia's Shi'a community in the oil-rich Eastern Province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qatif.jpg" alt="Qatif" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atheer Al-Sadah · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qatif_water_tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
